    "judgment": {
      "status": "confirmed",
      "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-126995: the direction vector has length sqrt(14), so the required distance gives |t|=1 and points (-1,1,7), (5,5,5). q-77443: the direction vector has length 3sqrt(3), again giving |t|=1 and points (2,0,6), (0,2,-4). q-162167: P+t(Q-P) gives a=-3, b=2, c=0, hence -1. q-162165: a=2, b=1, c=0, hence 3. q-16196: x²+y²=4 and the parameter traverses the entire origin-centered circle of radius 2; option 0 matches the source plot, which additionally marks sample points. q-16195: eliminating t gives y²=x³/25 with 0≤x≤16 and endpoints (16,±12.8); option 4 matches the source plot without its sample-point markers. These two source image paths differ from the choice paths but depict the same respective curves. q-123763: x increases from 6 without bound. q-123769: t≥1 gives x≤2 and y≥3, with both endpoints attained. q-123775: cosine and sine attain both extrema on the given parameter interval, yielding -1/2≤x≤1/2 and 2≤y≤4. q-158447: normalization gives 24/k=1, so k=24, which also ensures nonnegativity. q-158437: normalization gives 2/3+2k=1, so k=1/6, also ensuring nonnegativity. q-12049: QT=QP+PT=a-b. q-143001: I and III telescope to DF, whereas II equals FD. q-118976: I reverses direction, II telescopes to DB, and III equals AF rather than FA. q-11463 and q-126136: transposition exchanges rows and columns without changing entry signs. The supplied evidence contains worked solutions, not submitted-answer grades; each MA attribution below uses only its owning question's solution.",
